
  <sn><md>### Liming of Soil

**Definition:**
Liming is the **application of calcium and magnesium compounds** to acidic soil to **neutralize acidity**, raise pH, and improve soil fertility and crop growth.

---

**Materials Used:**

| Material | Formula | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Calcium carbonate** | CaCO₃ | Most common, mild, safe |
| **Calcium hydroxide** | Ca(OH)₂ | Fast acting, caustic |
| **Calcium oxide** | CaO | Quick lime, very fast, caustic |
| **Dolomite** | CaMg(CO₃)₂ | Also supplies Mg |
| **Wood ash** | — | Contains Ca, K, Mg |

---

**Reactions:**

**1. Neutralization of H⁺:**
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{CaCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{CO}_2
$$

**2. Detoxification of Al³⁺:**
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{Al}^{3+} + 3\text{CaCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - 3\text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{Al(OH)}_3 + 3\text{CO}_2
$$
Al³⁺ precipitates as non-toxic Al(OH)₃.

---

**Effects of Liming:**

**1. Raises Soil pH:**
- Neutralizes H⁺ and Al³⁺ → pH increases toward 6.5–7.0.

**2. Reduces Al and Mn Toxicity:**
- Al³⁺ and Mn²⁺ precipitate → no root damage.

**3. Increases Nutrient Availability:**
- P, Mo, Ca, Mg become available.
- N fixation and nitrification improve.

**4. Improves Soil Structure:**
- Ca²⁺ flocculates clay → better aggregation, aeration, and drainage.

**5. Enhances Microbial Activity:**
- Bacteria and actinomycetes work best at pH 6.0–7.5.

**6. Increases CEC and Base Saturation:**
- Ca²⁺ replaces H⁺ and Al³⁺ on exchange sites.

---

**Lime Requirement:**

Depends on:
- **Buffer pH** (measures potential acidity)
- **Soil texture** (clay &gt; sand)
- **Organic matter** (high OM → more lime)
- **Target pH** (usually 6.0–7.0 for most crops)
- **Crop requirement**

**Formula:**
$$
\text{Lime requirement} = \text{Exchangeable acidity} + \text{Residual acidity}
$$

---

**Factors Affecting Liming:**

| Factor | Effect |
|---|---|
| Soil texture | Clay soil needs more lime |
| Organic matter | High OM → more lime |
| Initial pH | Lower pH → more lime |
| Lime type | CaO &gt; Ca(OH)₂ &gt; CaCO₃ (fineness) |
| Fineness | Finer lime → faster reaction |
| Crop | Different crops need different pH |

---

**Methods of Application:**
- **Broadcasting** — spread evenly and incorporate
- **Band placement** — near root zone
- **Split application** — for high lime requirement
- Apply **2–3 months before sowing** for best results

---

**Precautions:**
- Avoid **over-liming** (pH &gt; 7.5 → micronutrient deficiency)
- Do not mix lime with **ammonium fertilizers** (volatilization loss)
- Use **dolomite** if Mg is also deficient
- Test soil before liming

---

**Example:**
In **Assam tea gardens**, soil pH is 4.5 due to heavy rainfall and ammonium fertilizers. Applying **CaCO₃ at 2–4 t/ha** raises pH to 5.5–6.0, reduces Al³⁺ toxicity, and improves tea yield.

**Conclusion:**
Liming is the **application of Ca/Mg compounds** to acidic soil to raise pH, reduce Al/Mn toxicity, improve nutrient availability, and enhance soil structure and microbial activity. Lime requirement depends on **buffer pH, texture, organic matter, and crop**. It is the most important practice for reclaiming acidic soils.</md></sn>
